#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231792} :activity/id #uuid "ae3b271d-f536-5674-bc90-24bf860c235c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050226 :topic/id #uuid "37af8af0-066f-5b4b-80e9-c7639425b063" :topic/math-academy-id 6726} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231792 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231991 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we will use Kirchhoff's voltage law to derive a second-order differential equation that governs the current $I(t)$ in an RCL circuit.\n\n![](images/d1/d1ce9424aca366d9028319fa2035e01339b26963e39150a8b14b8f2b34d67138.png)\n\nIn a series RCL circuit, Kirchhoff's voltage law states that the source voltage equals the sum of the voltage drops: $$E(t)=V_R+V_C+V_L$$\n\nFor the three components:\n\n- Resistor: the voltage drop is proportional to the current, $$V_R=RI.$$\n- Capacitor: the charge $q$ and capacitor voltage satisfy $q=CV_C,$ so $$V_C=\\dfrac{q}{C}.$$\n- Inductor: the voltage drop is proportional to the rate of change of current, $$V_L=L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.$$\n\nSubstituting these into Kirchhoff's voltage law gives $$E=RI+\\dfrac{q}{C}+L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.$$\n\nTo obtain a differential equation in terms of $I$ only, we first differentiate both sides with respect to $t{:}$ $$\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(E) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(RI+\\frac{q}{C}+L\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ \\frac{dE}{dt} & =R\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+\\frac{1}{C}\\cdot \\frac{dq}{dt}+L\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nNow, recall that the current $I(t),$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q(t),$ measured in coulombs: $$I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}=I,$ we get the second-order differential equation governing the current: $$L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+R\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\dfrac{1}{C}I=\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}E}{\\mathrm{d}t}$$\n\nFinally, dividing both sides by $L$ gives an equivalent form: $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+\\dfrac{R}{L}\\cdot\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\dfrac{1}{LC}I=\\dfrac{1}{L} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}E}{\\mathrm{d}t}$$\n\nLet's see a more concrete derivation in the next example." :tutorial/id #uuid "e9875681-c31f-535a-b3e2-7fcec32a16e3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14791 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "09eed649-fd35-5633-ab45-26f9ff4df9a4" :step/math-academy-id 602721 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231793}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231793 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231407 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231297 :question/id #uuid "20c07bb4-e264-5674-a7f0-9a833f68d037" :question/math-academy-id "e-21894"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "459aac20-b2e4-5d77-b131-717c9745d43c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048396}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372911} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372946}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Equation Governing the Charge in an RCL Circuit"} :step/id #uuid "7d00c846-017e-5944-a878-8692d8e20406" :step/math-academy-id 602722 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231796}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231794 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231408 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231298 :question/id #uuid "6f2eeea4-7339-5f96-b3e0-fa160a791034" :question/math-academy-id "e-21895"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6faa97d-4d8e-5d67-bb99-8aae6de5a840" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048130}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372932}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF"} :step/id #uuid "6b69ecfa-55d3-5851-a962-974fd7edd06b" :step/math-academy-id 602725 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231795}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231795 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231409 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231299 :question/id #uuid "cc9247b8-2a3d-5559-af6a-0cf1d6fc7a96" :question/math-academy-id "e-21896"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2fbf2a2-d7d1-5b6e-9304-38d9ae1c60bb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050378}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231584} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231585} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385934}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Equation Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit: Sinusoidal EMF"} :step/id #uuid "dfab021e-6bf7-5ab4-8bd9-2af9f811ab5b" :step/math-academy-id 602729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231796 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231994 :tutorial/content "When we solve a second-order differential equation for the current $I(t),$ we often write the solution in the form $$I(t)=I_c(t)+I_p(t),$$ where\n\n- $I_c(t)$ is the **transient current**, which decays to $0$ as $t\\to\\infty,$ and\n- $I_p(t)$ is the **steady-state current**, which governs the current’s long-term behavior.\n\nTo determine a unique solution for $I(t),$ we must specify two initial conditions, typically $I(0)$ and $I'(0).$\n\nThe current is often given directly as an initial condition, for example, $I(0)=I_0.$ We are also often given the initial *capacitor charge*, $q(0)=q_0.$\n\nTo find the other condition $I'(0),$ we use Kirchhoff's voltage law equation: $$E(t) = RI+\\dfrac{q}{C}+L\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$$\n\nSolving for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ gives $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = \\dfrac{1}{L}E - \\dfrac{R}{L}I - \\dfrac{q}{LC}.$$\n\nNow evaluate at $t=0{:}$ $$I'(0) = \\dfrac{1}{L}E(0) - \\dfrac{R}{L}I(0) - \\dfrac{1}{LC}q(0)$$\n\nSo, if the initial charge $q(0)$ and initial current $I(0)$ are given, we can compute $I'(0)$ by substituting those values into the formula above.\n\nIn the next slide, we'll determine the initial conditions of a differential equation that governs the current in a given circuit." :tutorial/id #uuid "a6f06e46-ed1e-5e49-a9f8-679033a08a0f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14808 :tutorial/title "Initial Conditions for Solving the ODE Governing the Current in an RCL Circuit"} :step/id #uuid "10bd959b-3db6-58ef-8e40-eabd05f87aab" :step/math-academy-id 603155 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186232020 :tutorial/content "Suppose a circuit with a constant EMF of $E=8\\,\\textrm{V},$ resistance of $R=6\\,\\Omega,$ capacitance of $C=0.25\\,\\textrm{F},$ and inductance of $L=2\\,\\textrm{H},$ satisfies Kirchhoff's voltage law equation $$8=6I+4q+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}.$$\n\nFirst, let's derive the differential equation for $I$ by differentiating both sides with respect to $t{:}$ $$0=6\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+4\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}.$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}q}{\\mathrm{d}t}=I,$ the differential equation for $I$ is $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2I=0.$$\n\nNow suppose the initial charge and current are $q(0)=\\dfrac12$ and $I(0)=1,$ respectively. Solving our first relation for $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t} = 4-3I-2q.$$\n\nThen, the initial conditions for the current we use to solve the ODE are $I(0)=1$ and $$\\begin{aligned}{I}^{'}(0) & =4-3I(0)-2q(0) \\\\ & =4-3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ & =0.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nSolving the resulting initial value problem $$\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}^2I}{\\mathrm{d}t^2}+3\\,\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}I}{\\mathrm{d}t}+2I=0, \\qquad I(0) = 1, \\quad I'(0) = 0,$$ we obtain $$I(t)=2e^{-t}-e^{-2t}.$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "02b0c1dd-4819-5337-bc05-870ded85d834" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14901 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "a6ed1a27-61c7-514f-ab23-a91a7edba38d" :step/math-academy-id 608077 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186231794}}] :activity/title "Modeling Current in RCL Circuits With Second-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186231523}]}
